:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the central rule content wrong. It says Rule 6c-11 does not list permitted AP counterparties, but Rule 6c-11(a)(1) defines an AP as a member or participant of an SEC-registered clearing agency with a written agreement with the ETF or one of its service providers. That definition, including the clearing-agency eligibility and the written-agreement counterparties, is never stated. It also misframes the permitted counterparties as 'broker-dealers acting under the fund's arrangements,' when the rule refers to the ETF or its service providers. It abstains on the reviewed forms, so it omits that the distributor and AP are the main counterparties, the transfer agent countersigns, and the trust is a third-party beneficiary that sometimes signs for specified provisions. Its guess that the forms add a custodian is unsupported. Its NSCC/DTC remarks are generic ('CNS/DTC participants') and do not state the forms' requirement of NSCC netting membership for the clearing process and/or DTC participation outside it. It does not note that eligibility is distinct from being a market maker.\"}","ttft_ms":2777,"type":"result","duration_ms":5850,"uuid":"45635fd8-bd42-40d8-bec6-5f8c295d72a5","ttft_stream_ms":699,"time_to_request_ms":92,"first_content_frame_ms":699,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
